    <node><name>0</name><description>What is wrong</description><answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest><sans>ans1</sans><tans>ta1</tans><testoptions></testoptions><quiet>0</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>1.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-1-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>1</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-1-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
    <node><name>1</name><description>Said Kiprono is right</description><answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest><sans>ans1</sans><tans>"ok"</tans><testoptions></testoptions><quiet>0</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-2-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>[[if test="is(er=1)"]]Every point of his line is \({@d@}\) units from \({@vs@} = {@c@}\). Now test a point that is not on his line, such as one with \({@vs@} = {@c-d@}\): how far is it from \({@vs@} = {@c@}\)?[[elif test="is(er=2)"]]Test a point on one of his lines, such as one with \({@vs@} = {@exd(c+d/2)@}\): is it \({@d@}\) units from \({@vs@} = {@c@}\)?[[else]]Test a point on one of his lines, such as one with \({@vs@} = {@d@}\): is it \({@d@}\) units from \({@vs@} = {@c@}\)?[[/if]]</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>-1</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-2-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>[[if test="is(ans1=\"one\")"]]Kiprono has given two lines, not one. Work out how far each of his lines is from \({@vs@} = {@c@}\).[[elif test="is(ans1=\"half\") and is(er=1)"]]Half the distance would be \({@exd(d/2)@}\) [[if test="is(d=2)"]]unit[[else]]units[[/if]]. His line \({@vs@} = {@c+d@}\) is \({@d@}\) units from \({@vs@} = {@c@}\), the full distance. Is one line the whole locus?[[elif test="is(ans1=\"half\")"]]Half the distance would put his lines \({@exd(d/2)@}\) [[if test="is(d=2)"]]unit[[else]]units[[/if]] from \({@vs@} = {@c@}\). His lines \({@vs@} = {@d@}\) and \({@vs@} = {@-d@}\) are \({@abs(d-c)@}\) and \({@abs(d+c)@}\) units from it, which are not equal.[[elif test="is(er=1)"]]His line \({@vs@} = {@c+d@}\) is \({@d@}\) units from \({@vs@} = {@c@}\), so he did measure from the line. Is one line the whole locus?[[else]]His lines are each \({@exd(d/2)@}\) [[if test="is(d=2)"]]unit[[else]]units[[/if]] from \({@vs@} = {@c@}\), one on each side of it, so he did measure from the line. Compare that distance with the \({@d@}\) units asked for.[[/if]]</p>]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
  </prt>
  <prt><name>prt2</name><value>1.0000000</value><autosimplify>1</autosimplify><feedbackstyle>1</feedbackstyle><feedbackvariables><text><![CDATA[sa: sort([ans2, ans3]);
e0: abs(sa[1]-(c-d))+abs(sa[2]-(c+d));
eh: abs(sa[1]-hf[1])+abs(sa[2]-hf[2]);
el: abs(sa[1]-hl[1])+abs(sa[2]-hl[2]);
one: is(abs(ans2-(c-d))<0.001 or abs(ans2-(c+d))<0.001 or abs(ans3-(c-d))<0.001 or abs(ans3-(c+d))<0.001);
nd(v) := if integerp(v) then v else float(floor(1000*v+1/2)/1000);]]></text></feedbackvariables>
